{"slug": "pt100-rtd-calibration-guide", "targets": ["rtd resistance formula", "rtd pt100 formula", "rtd formula pt100"], "html": "<h2 style=\"font-size:24px;font-weight:700;color:#0186e0;margin:34px 0 16px;\">RTD Resistance Formula: Callendar Van Dusen Equation for PT100</h2><p><strong>The RTD resistance formula</strong> used for accurate PT100 work is the Callendar Van Dusen equation. The linear form Rt = R0 (1 + &alpha;t) is good for quick checks, but the full RTD PT100 formula adds square and fourth order terms that correct the slight curve of platinum.</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Range</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Equation</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0 to 850 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Rt = R0 (1 + At + Bt<sup>2</sup>)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">minus 200 to 0 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">Rt = R0 [1 + At + Bt<sup>2</sup> + C(t minus 100)t<sup>3</sup>]</td></tr></table></div><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Coefficient</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Value for &alpha; = 0.00385 platinum</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">R0</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 &Omega; at 0 &deg;C</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">A</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">3.9083 x 10<sup>-3</sup> /&deg;C</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">B</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">minus 5.775 x 10<sup>-7</sup> /&deg;C<sup>2</sup></td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">minus 4.183 x 10<sup>-12</sup> /&deg;C<sup>4</sup> (below 0 &deg;C only)</td></tr></table></div><h3 style=\"font-size:19px;font-weight:700;color:#1a2b4a;margin:20px 0 10px;\">Worked Values Using the RTD Formula PT100 Coefficients</h3><div style=\"overflow-x:auto;\"><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:14px 0;font-size:16px;\"><tr><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Temperature</th><th style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;background:#0186e0;color:#fff;\">Resistance</th></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">minus 100 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">60.26 &Omega;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">0 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100.00 &Omega;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">100 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">138.51 &Omega;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">200 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">175.86 &Omega;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">300 &deg;C</td><td style=\"border:1px solid #cfe3f5;padding:8px 10px;text-align:left;\">212.05 &Omega;</td></tr></table></div><p>At 300 &deg;C the linear formula gives 215.5 &Omega;, about 3.4 &Omega; higher, so always use the full equation or a standard table when calibrating above 100 &deg;C.</p>"}